--TEST--
`Core\Response::html` writes a `Core\Html\Markup`'s bytes as they are, which is the whole of what taking the carrier buys — `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member` and `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4's HTML row takes the carrier and so has nothing to refuse: every door
// into a `Markup` already decided what may be raw, so this member escapes
// nothing and the page comes out as the handler built it. The
// `text/html; charset=utf-8` it declares beside the write is not observable
// off a request — `each_body_member_sets_its_own_content_type` in
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/response.rs` is where that half is asserted, and gap
// 1 of that module owns why the bytes still land here.
Core\Html\Markup $page = html`<p>hello & welcome</p>`;
Core\Response::html($page);
echo "\n";

// What it does *not* do, over the identical characters: the launderer is the
// path text takes into the carrier, and it writes them as references. The two
// lines together are the bound on either side, so a member that quietly
// escaped its argument fails on the first and one that quietly stopped
// escaping fails on the second.
Core\Response::html(Core\Html::escape("<p>hello & welcome</p>"));
echo "\n";

// A literal's hole is escaped once, where `rule:core-classes/html-template`
// escapes it, and this member does not escape it a second time — which is the
// failure the carrier exists to make unwritable, since escaping is not
// idempotent.
var $name = "ada & <grace>";
Core\Response::html(html`<b>{$name}</b>`);
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
<p>hello & welcome</p>
&lt;p&gt;hello &amp; welcome&lt;/p&gt;
<b>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</b>
